roll services solution; integrated payroll; benefits administration; HR administration and employee onboarding; time and attendance; and performance management services. The company was formerly known as Administaff, Inc. and changed its name to Insperity, Inc. in March 2011. Insperity, Inc. was founded in 1986 and is headquartered in Kingwood, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Paul J. Sarvadi | Co-Founder, Chairman & CEO | Age 68 | Compensation: $2.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 305K people. Revenue per employee of $22K ranks in the bottom quartile among Industrials peers, with revenue growing 10%/yr across its filed history. Stock-based compensation of $200 per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 101\u00d7 revenue per employee (high relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (3/10).","financial_summary":["Insperity, Inc. is a small-cap industrials business with a $1.9B market cap on $6.8B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +5% over the past three years and +10% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Gross margins of 17% are the starting point for assessing pricing power.","The company is burning through $309M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","Accruals are elevated at +12.3% of assets, and Sloan showed firms building earnings through accruals rather than cash systematically underperform.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year, and thin -0.4\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 5.8% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 6.2% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Accruals elevated at +12.3% of assets \u2014 Sloan showed firms building earnings through accruals rather than cash systematically underperform","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Piotroski score 1/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Beneish M-Score flags potential earnings manipulation \u2014 a question of whether the reported numbers can be trusted","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio looks stretched \u2014 the dividend\u2019s own margin of safety is thin","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Operating margins 9% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Carries a 173% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.7286059872996673},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 8.1% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Financial quality metrics lag Industrials peers","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","Founder-led \u2014 owner alignment that often matters more than any incentive plan","Large open-market insider purchases (net $15.5M) \u2014 management putting its own capital behind the business","21 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Among the least levered in Industrials \u2014 the fortress balance sheet in its peer set"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Can NSP's Margin Recovery Plan Fuel a Sustaina